---
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import ReleaseBlock from "../components/ReleaseBlock.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { appBySlug, repoUrl } from "../data/apps";
import { archiveFolder, assetByKind } from "../data/release-model";
import { releaseFor } from "../data/releases";
import { url } from "../paths";

const app = appBySlug("ftp");
const release = releaseFor("ftp");
const archive = release ? assetByKind(release, "archive") : undefined;
const repo = repoUrl;
---

<h1 class="app-title">
  <img src={url("rivet-ftp.svg")} width="48" height="48" alt="" />
  Rivet FTP
</h1>
<StatusBadge app={app} release={release} />
<p>
  A dual-pane file-transfer app. This computer on one side, the server on the other, and a
  transfer queue. It is written in Rust, draws with egui, and does not use GTK or Qt. The window
  is also the connection manager for the <code>rivet-ftp</code> command, and for plain
  <code>ssh</code> once you add one Include line.
</p>
<p>
  {release?.prerelease && "Early alpha. "}
  {release ? (
    <>
      The latest download is <a href={release.htmlUrl}>{release.version}</a>, published {release.dateLabel}.
      {release.prerelease && " GitHub marks it as a pre-release."}
    </>
  ) : (
    <>No release is published yet.</>
  )}
  {" "}This version speaks SFTP. FTP and FTPS addresses can be saved. Opening them is not built yet.
  {release?.prerelease && " Things may still change or break. Try it on servers and files you have backups of."}
</p>

<Shot
  name="ftp-connected-wide.png"
  alt="Rivet FTP connected to a server, with this computer and the server side by side and a few finished transfers"
  caption="Connected, with finished transfers. The pictures use a demo server made of a local folder. No network, and no ssh daemon."
  eager
/>

<nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
  <a href="#window">The window</a>
  <a href="#transfers">Transfers</a>
  <a href="#editing">Editing</a>
  <a href="#ssh">SSH keys</a>
  <a href="#connections">Saved connections</a>
  <a href="#install">Install</a>
  <a href="#keys">Keys</a>
</nav>

<h2 id="window">The window</h2>
<p>
  Saved connections sit in groups in the sidebar. A group can fold. Tabs hold several servers at
  once. Each tab has its own panes and queue, and a tab in the background keeps transferring.
  The quick-connect bar takes <code>user@server</code> or
  <code>sftp://user@server:port/folder</code>. <code>user@server</code> alone means SFTP.
</p>
<p>
  Both panes browse on their own. Each has Rivet's stepped path bar, columns you can sort by name,
  size, or date, and hidden files (Ctrl+H). Drag between the panes, double-click a file to send it
  across, or use Upload and Download. The toolbar can be arranged, and the menu bar can be hidden.
  The window follows the desktop's colors, fonts, and icon theme, in light or dark.
</p>
<Shot
  name="ftp-light-wide.png"
  alt="Rivet FTP in the light theme, with both panes showing the same site"
  caption="The same window in the light theme."
/>
<Shot
  name="ftp-new-connection-wide.png"
  alt="The New Connection window, with SFTP selected and a name of Work site"
  caption="A new connection. It is saved for the window and for rivet-ftp in a terminal."
/>

<h2 id="transfers">Transfers</h2>
<p>
  Files and whole folders go into a queue. A download shows how much has moved, the speed, and the
  time left. An upload shows the time so far: sftp, in the batch mode Rivet uses, does not report
  upload progress. A finished row shows the size, the time, and the speed. A failed transfer can
  be retried.
</p>
<p>
  When a file is already on the other side, the dates and sizes are compared first. The window can
  keep the copy already there, replace the older files, or replace all of them. Preferences,
  Transfers can set that choice to ask, replace, or skip. Double-click can send the file across,
  or only select it. Pause and resume, a bandwidth limit, several transfers at once, and folder
  sync are still on the list.
</p>
<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="ftp-replace-wide.png"
    alt="A dialog comparing two items that are already on the server, with Keep Theirs, Replace Older, and Replace All"
    caption="Already on the server. Dates and sizes are compared before anything is replaced."
  />
  <Shot
    name="ftp-preferences-wide.png"
    alt="Preferences, Transfers: ask, replace, or skip when a file is already there, and the editor for server files"
    caption="Preferences, Transfers. Ask, replace, or skip, and which editor opens a server file."
  />
</div>

<h2 id="editing">Editing and file info</h2>
<p>
  Right-click a file on the server and choose Open in Editor (or press F4), or Open With to pick
  an app. Rivet FTP downloads it to a private folder, opens it, and sends each save back. If the
  server copy changed, it asks before replacing it. The files open for editing are listed above
  the transfers. Stop ends it and deletes the local copy. Preferences, Transfers chooses the
  editor: one app, or the default for each type.
</p>
<p>
  Info shows size, date, owner, and permissions, including chmod, and the same for everything in a
  folder. New folder, rename, and delete work on the server. Delete asks first. Preferences says a
  file deleted on a server cannot be brought back. Pictures and text can be previewed. A server
  file is downloaded to a private temporary folder first.
</p>
<Shot
  name="ftp-info-wide.png"
  alt="Info for index.html on the server, with owner, size, and permission checkboxes"
  caption="Info and permissions for a file on the server."
/>

<h2 id="ssh">SSH keys</h2>
<p>
  SFTP goes through the system's own <code>sftp</code> program, so keys, the ssh agent,
  <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, and known_hosts apply. A new server is trusted on first use. A
  changed server key is refused.
</p>
<p>
  Preferences, SSH Keys lists the keys in <code>~/.ssh</code>, can make a new Ed25519 or RSA 4096
  key, with an optional passphrase, can copy a public key, and can add one to a server with
  <code>ssh-copy-id</code> in a terminal. Each connection's settings pick its key. Private keys
  stay in <code>~/.ssh</code>. A key with a passphrase needs <code>ssh-add</code> before
  connecting. With no key chosen, ssh tries the agent and its usual keys.
</p>
<pre><code>rivet-ftp keys
rivet-ftp add Pi pi@192.168.1.4 --key id_ed25519</code></pre>
<Shot
  name="ftp-keys-wide.png"
  alt="Preferences, SSH Keys, listing id_ed25519 with Add to Server and Copy Public Key"
  caption="Preferences, SSH Keys. Add to Server sends the public half."
/>

<h2 id="connections">Saved connections</h2>
<p>
  Passwords are never written into the connections file. If you ask the window to remember a
  password or a key passphrase, it goes in the desktop keyring. SSH logins use keys or the ssh
  agent.
</p>
<pre><code>rivet-ftp add "Work site" sftp://me@example.com:2222/var/www --key ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 --local ~/Sites/work
rivet-ftp add Pi pi@192.168.1.4
rivet-ftp add Mirror ftps://files.example.org/pub --category Clients
rivet-ftp list
rivet-ftp show work-site
rivet-ftp remove Pi</code></pre>
<p>
  Addresses look like <code>sftp://user@server:port/folder</code>, <code>ftp://</code>, or
  <code>ftps://</code>. <code>--category</code> puts the connection in a group.
</p>
<pre><code>rivet-ftp ssh work-site
rivet-ftp ssh work-site git status
rivet-ftp sftp work-site
rivet-ftp url work-site
rivet-ftp ssh work-site --print</code></pre>
<p>
  Every save also writes <code>~/.config/rivet-ftp/ssh_config</code>. One line near the top of
  <code>~/.ssh/config</code> lets <code>ssh</code>, <code>scp</code>, <code>rsync</code>, and
  <code>git</code> use those names:
</p>
<pre><code>Include ~/.config/rivet-ftp/ssh_config</code></pre>
<p>
  Connections live in <code>~/.config/rivet-ftp/connections.conf</code>, plain text, readable only
  by you. You can edit it by hand. <code>rivet-ftp ssh-config</code> prints the Include line again.
</p>
<pre><code>[Work site]
protocol=sftp
host=example.com
port=2222
user=me
key=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
remote=/var/www
local=~/Sites/work</code></pre>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<ReleaseBlock slug="ftp" />
{
  archive && (
    <>
      <p>
        Unpack the archive and run <code>install.sh</code>. That puts <code>rivet-ftp</code> in
        <code>~/.local/bin</code> and adds Rivet FTP to the app launcher.
        {!release?.assets.some((item) => item.kind === "appimage" || item.kind === "flatpak") &&
          " There is no AppImage or Flatpak for this release."}
      </p>
      <pre>
        <code>{`tar xzf ${archive.name}\ncd ${archiveFolder(archive.name)}\n./install.sh`}</code>
      </pre>
    </>
  )
}
<p>
  The program is a portable x86_64 build, linked against glibc 2.31. The window needs
  <a href={url("files/#libraries")}>the same libraries as Rivet Files</a>.
</p>
<h3>From source</h3>
<p>
  Rust 1.88 or newer. From the top of the repository,
  <code>utilities/rivet-dev install ftp</code> builds a release and adds the launcher entry. Or:
</p>
<pre><code>cargo build --release -p rivet-ftp
./target/release/rivet-ftp
./target/release/rivet-ftp open work-site
./target/release/rivet-ftp --help
./target/release/rivet-ftp --install-desktop-entry</code></pre>
<p>
  Still ahead, from the feature checklist: opening FTP and FTPS, pause and resume, more than one
  transfer at a time, folder sync, reconnecting after a drop, and Rivet's icon and compact views
  inside the panes. The checklist is
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-ftp-parity.md`}>docs/rivet-ftp-parity.md</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="keys">Keys</h2>
<p>The project notes list these. They are also in the menus, and in Help, Keyboard Shortcuts.</p>
<dl class="keys">
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Tab</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Switch between the panes.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Enter</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Open a folder, or send a file to the other side. Double-click does this too.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Backspace</kbd> <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Up a folder. Alt+Home is the home folder.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Up</kbd> <kbd>Down</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Move through the list. Shift extends the selection.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Select everything.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>F4</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Open a server file in an editor. Saves go back to the server.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>U</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Upload or download the selection.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>F2</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Rename.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Delete</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Delete on the server, after a confirmation.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd></dt>
    <dd>New folder.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Type a path.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd></dt>
    <dd>New connection. Quick connect.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Disconnect.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Show hidden files.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> <kbd>F9</kbd> <kbd>F8</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Show or hide the menu bar, connections, transfers.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>F5</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Refresh.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>W</kbd></dt>
    <dd>New tab, close the tab.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Next or previous tab. Ctrl+Page Down and Ctrl+Page Up do this too.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>,</kbd></dt>
    <dd>Preferences.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<p>
  Longer notes: <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-ftp.md`}>docs/rivet-ftp.md</a>,
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-ftp/README.md`}>apps/rivet-ftp</a>, and the
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-ftp/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a>.
</p>
